# PyAV: video poster-frame extraction for VideoObject structured data.
# The manylinux wheel STATICALLY BUNDLES FFmpeg's shared libraries, so this is
# a pure requirements addition -- `docker/py3base/Dockerfile` installs no apt
# packages and does not need to. Verified to install, import, encode and decode
# the then-pinned base image (python:3.10-bullseye) AND on 3.12, which is
# what both CI and the image (python:3.12-bookworm, the application contract) now run; the
# transcript is in the the application contract PR body. A version without a cp312 wheel would
# try to compile from source, and that image has no libav*-dev -- the image
# build would fail at deploy time with a green gate, because CI never builds
# cloudbuild.yaml.
#
# **Pinned to the NEWEST version, on purpose: this is a native media decoder
# fed attacker-supplied bytes.** 16.0.1 bundles libavcodec 62.11.100 (the
# FFmpeg 8.0 series); 13.1.0 bundled 61.3.100 (FFmpeg 7.0), which carries
# published decoder CVEs. Bumping this pin is a security action, not a chore.
# Note what a pip pin CANNOT tell you: which FFmpeg POINT release is inside
# the wheel. `av.library_versions` reports the library soname versions only.
# So the real mitigation is not the pin -- it is
# `uploads.video_utils.ALLOWED_VIDEO_CODECS`, which refuses to hand a frame to
# any of FFmpeg's ~200 decoders except the handful a browser can play.
av==19.0.1
Django==5.2.18
# channels/channels_redis must be upgraded together. channels 4.2.1 renamed
# BaseChannelLayer.valid_channel_name/valid_group_name -> require_valid_*
# "without deprecation" (upstream considered them internal), which broke every
# channels_redis <= 4.2.1 group_add/receive with
# `AttributeError: 'RedisChannelLayer' object has no attribute
# 'valid_channel_name'` -- i.e. every WebSocket connect failed in prod.
# channels_redis 4.3.0 uses the new names and declares `channels>=4.2.2`.
# Do not downgrade either pin below these versions.
channels==4.3.2
channels_redis==4.3.0
# DIRECT dependency of `dj/apps/ktauth/signing.py`, which imports
# `cryptography.hazmat.primitives.asymmetric.ed25519` at module scope for the
# KT authority key. It has never been pinned here, and until now it did not
# need to be: `django-rest-knox` 4.2.0 required it outright (its
# `SECURE_HASH_ALGORITHM` default was
# `cryptography.hazmat.primitives.hashes.SHA512`).
#
# **knox 5.x drops `cryptography` in favour of `hashlib`.** What is left
# holding it in the image is a set of packages that merely happen to want it
# today -- `webauthn>=49.0.0`, `pyOpenSSL<51,>=49.0.0`, `service-identity>=47`,
# `sendgrid`, `autobahn`, `google-auth` -- none of which this repo pins for
# that reason. A bump that dropped the last of them would remove KT signing's
# dependency with a green gate: CI never builds `docker/py3base/Dockerfile`,
# and `ImportError` at module scope means every ktauth request 500s at deploy.
# So it is named explicitly, as the direct dependency it actually is.
#
# 50.0.1 is what the current requirement set already resolves to; it also
# satisfies pyOpenSSL's `<51` ceiling, so this pin does not fight a transitive
# constraint. Raise it deliberately -- it is a compiled crypto primitive, so a
# bump is a security action, and `pyOpenSSL`'s upper bound must move first.
cryptography==50.0.2
