# Interactive shell only (django_extensions `shell_plus` picks it up if it is
# importable). NOTHING in the application imports it -- the only two mentions
# in the tree are commented-out `import IPython; IPython.embed()` debugger
# lines. 7.31.1 carried GHSA-29gw-9793-fvw7 / CVE-2023-24816 (command
# injection through `IPython.utils.terminal.set_term_title` on a
# non-Windows terminal), fixed in 8.10.0.
#
# 9.x needs Python >=3.11. That is why the the application contract bump to 9.17.1 failed: the
# audit and the image were still on 3.10 and pip could not resolve it at all.
# docker/py3base/Dockerfile is python:3.12-bookworm since the application contract and the
# `dependency-security` job resolves under the same 3.12, so the constraint
# that blocked it is gone -- verified by resolving main.txt under 3.12 here.
ipython==9.17.1
jsonlines==4.0.0
# BCP-47 validation + CLDR canonicalization for the language registry
# (f2z/languages.py, the application contract). Pure-Python `py3-none-any` wheel, 183 KB, and
# **zero runtime dependencies** -- it bundles the IANA subtag registry as a
# text file. Requires-Python >=3.9, so it is fine on the 3.12 image.
#
# Deliberately NOT `langcodes[data]`: the `data` extra adds `language_data`
# (5.6 MB wheel, ~21 MB installed) and `marisa-trie`, a C extension. When
# this was decided CI ran 3.12 and the image ran 3.10, and marisa-trie shipped
# no cp310 wheel, so the two would have resolved *different* binaries with CI
# never building cloudbuild.yaml to notice. The interpreters agree now,
# but a compiled extension nothing at runtime needs is still not worth the
# image. The CLDR display names we need are generated at build time by
# bin/gen-language-names.py and committed as f2z/language_names.py instead.
langcodes==3.5.1
